[THOUGHT]  
The patient reiterates the core visual symptoms (decreased vision + floaters in right eye ×2 days) but adds the self-interpretation “I think they are floaters” — clinically useful, as true floaters often correlate with vitreous opacities or retinal pathology (e.g., hemorrhage, inflammation). No new systemic or dermatologic findings are reported: no rash (palmar/soleal/truncal), no mucocutaneous ulcers, no alopecia, no hearing changes. This further narrows the differential away from syphilis (though RPR/TPPA remain essential given ocular involvement in late neurosyphilis), sarcoidosis, or vasculitides that commonly present with skin/mucosal manifestations. The fundoscopic findings — **granular yellow-white perivascular opacities + dot-blot hemorrhages** — remain highly specific for **CMV retinitis**, especially with CD4 = 36/mm³. The absence of pain, photopsia, or curtain effect makes rhegmatogenous retinal detachment less likely, though OCT is still needed to exclude subtle schisis or early detachment. Slit-lamp remains pending to assess for anterior segment inflammation (e.g., cells/flare), which would suggest infectious uveitis (e.g., syphilis, TB, HSV) or IRIS — but the classic fundus appearance strongly favors CMV.

[SUMMARY]  
**Refined Clinical Assessment**:  
- **Diagnosis of concern**: **Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis**, AIDS-defining illness, supported by:  
  • Profound immunosuppression (CD4 = 36/mm³);  
  • Acute unilateral vision loss and floaters;  
  • Pathognomonic fundoscopic findings: granular yellow-white perivascular exudates (“frosted branch angiitis”-like appearance) and intraretinal dot-blot hemorrhages — reflecting viral cytopathic effect and microvascular damage.  
- **Key negatives reduce likelihood of mimics**:  
  • No rash, mucosal ulcers, alopecia, or hearing loss → lowers suspicion for syphilis, sarcoidosis, or autoimmune retinopathy;  
  • No photopsia, curtain, or pain → makes retinal detachment or acute optic neuropathy less likely;  
  • No systemic B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) → argues against active disseminated OIs or lymphoma.  
- **Critical pending tests**:  
  • **Serum CMV PCR**: Positive result supports diagnosis and guides therapy intensity;  
  • **RPR & TPPA**: Must be obtained *regardless* — ocular syphilis can mimic CMV retinitis and is fully treatable; false-negative RPR occurs in up to 30% of neurosyphilis cases, so TPPA is confirmatory;  
  • **OCT**: To evaluate for retinal thickening, edema, full-thickness necrosis, or subretinal fluid — distinguishing active CMV from healed scars or IRIS-related inflammation;  
  • **Slit-lamp exam**: To detect anterior chamber cells/flare (suggesting concurrent uveitis) or keratic precipitates (e.g., in sarcoid or herpes zoster).  
- **Urgent action required**:  
  • Immediate referral to **ocular infectious disease specialist or retina ophthalmologist** for diagnostic confirmation and initiation of **induction anti-CMV therapy** (e.g., oral valganciclovir 900 mg BID × 2–3 weeks) — delay risks irreversible retinal destruction and bilateral spread.  
  • Concurrently: optimize ART adherence counseling, check HIV viral load, and assess for other OI prophylaxis gaps.  

*Note*: While the patient reports “black spots” and interprets them as floaters, the associated dot-blot hemorrhages suggest these may represent actual vitreous hemorrhage or clumped inflammatory debris — reinforcing need for urgent vitreous assessment (e.g., B-scan ultrasound if media opacity limits view).

